  "generated_text": "To determine the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity, we start by solving the equation \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\).\n\nFirst, let's make a substitution to simplify the equation. Let \\( w = z^2 \\). Then the equation \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) becomes:\n\\[\nw^2 + w + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation for \\( w \\) using the quadratic formula:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\]\nwhere \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 1 \\), and \\( c = 1 \\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\( w \\) are:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad w = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the roots of \\( z^2 = w \\). For each \\( w \\), we solve for \\( z \\):\n\\[\nz = \\pm \\sqrt{w}\n\\]\n\nLet's consider \\( w = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\). We know that:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right) = e^{i\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\nThus, the square roots of \\( w \\) are:\n\\[\nz = \\pm \\sqrt{e^{i\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}} = \\pm e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for \\( w = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right) = e^{-i\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\nThus, the square roots of \\( w \\) are:\n\\[\nz = \\pm \\sqrt{e^{-i\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}} = \\pm e^{-i\\frac{\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\n\nCombining these, the roots of the original polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are:\n\\[\nz = e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{3}}, \\quad z = e^{-i\\frac{\\pi}{3}}, \\quad z = e^{i\\frac{5\\pi}{3}}, \\quad z = e^{-i\\frac{5\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\n\nWe recognize that \\( e^{i\\frac{5\\pi}{3}} = e^{-i\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\) and \\( e^{-i\\frac{5\\pi}{3}} = e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\). Therefore, the distinct roots are:\n\\[\nz = e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{3}}, \\quad z = e^{-i\\frac{\\pi}{3}}, \\quad z = e^{i\\pi}, \\quad z = e^{-i\\pi}\n\\]\n\nThese roots are the 6th roots of unity:\n\\[\n1, \\quad -1, \\quad e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{3}}, \\quad e^{-i\\frac{\\pi}{3}}, \\quad e^{i\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}, \\quad e^{-i\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
